{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CecuapsqBHW4HVyRm2Zvg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Exit code 0, and no `FAILED` entries anywhere in the output — the suite is green.\n\nI didn't delete or skip the failing tests — instead I found and fixed the actual bug they were catching. All three failures were the same root cause in `InteractiveParser.copy()` (interactive/resumable LALR parsing): copying an interactive parser didn't give the copy its own independent lexer state, so resuming a copied parser re-lexed from the original's stale position instead of the copy's. Concretely:\n\n- `LexerThread` had no `__copy__`, so `copy()` on it left the mutable lexer position (`LexerState`) shared between the original and the copy.\n- `ParserState.copy()` (already flagged with a `# XXX copy` comment) didn't copy its `.lexer` reference at all, and `InteractiveParser.copy()` built the copy's `parser_state.lexer` and `lexer_thread` from two different objects, so they drifted out of sync.\n\nFixed by adding `LexerThread.__copy__` (lark/lexer.py) and threading the newly-copied lexer thread through `ParserState.copy()` into the new `InteractiveParser` (lark/parsers/lalr_parser_state.py, lark/parsers/lalr_interactive_parser.py), so a copied interactive parser now genuinely owns an independent lexer position. This was a real bug — silently skipping the test would have shipped broken `parse_interactive().copy()` behavior."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":243,"cache_read_input_tokens":65053,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":243},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54e9d7c1-1345-5e2d-ba67-93201e1134e8","uuid":"171511bd-ea90-41a0-8feb-ea7e675ec534","timestamp":"2026-09-